+[NFR-RES-01](../reference/FR_NFR.md#35-resource-reliability-and-the-rest) in the [requirements](../reference/FR_NFR.md) is the accepted target.
+Mark II needs fewer than 20,000 `xc7a100t` LUTs at 32x32.
+That is fewer than 20,000 LUTs at 32x32.
+The image must also fit Block RAM, with timing met.
+Status: **F0 implemented behind a default-off build switch.**
+The all-fabric build is unchanged and remains the shipping image.
+This page designs the fabric's contract with the control-plane firmware.
+The split integrates before P3.
+Milestone 13 retains the hard-core port.
+Lane F3 adds the [ACMP module](#the-acmp-module) to the firmware.
+The host tests and the co-simulation build compile it.
+No image links that firmware yet.
+Lane F-INT adds the [publication block](#the-publication-block), contract 2.2.
+There the firmware owner writes the class-D state.
+The split placement's datapath reads that state.
+Nothing reads it until the placement switch lands.
+The [placement contract](../ARCHITECTURE_HW_SW_SPLIT.md) defines the transition and end state.
+The owner decisions of 2026-10-05 on [#640](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/640) define the interface.
+So does the directive on [#665](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/665).
+The numbers live in one place, the generated [reference page](../reference/MAILBOX_CONTRACT.md).
+This page explains them.
+The bare-metal core runs ADP, ACMP, MAAP, SRP and AECP.
+SRP runs through lwSRP.
+The fabric keeps framing, timestamps and the media path.
+It also keeps hard-deadline timers and an ingress filter.
+It keeps the gPTP plane until #665 lane F6.
+Then firmware owns the protocol and servo.
+The two exchange frames through packet mailboxes.
+Those are block-RAM rings with a doorbell per ring.
+There is one interrupt, 32-bit accesses only and no DMA.
+- **Event record:** four words. Its sources are link level changes and grandmaster changes. The gPTP plane publishes the latter until #665 lane F6. Its sources also include fabric timer expiries, tagged by arm. The centisecond tick is the fourth source.
+- **[Mark II levers](#mark-ii-levers)** -- Owner order, 32x32 additions, about 21.75k.
+- **[LiteX removal stages](#litex-removal-stages)** -- Three stages and every LiteX block's fate.
+- **[Mark II decisions](#mark-ii-decisions)** -- Owner and manager rulings, 2026-10-31 and 2026-11-02.
+- **Start estimate:** about 31.0k LUTs once the processor leaves.
+  The owner's 34.2k counted the CPU twice ([lever 4](#lever-4-accounting)).
+- **The six owner levers** then reach about 21.0k centrally.
+  Lever 4 saves nothing: it was that double count.
+  The central estimate lands near 21.75k, above the ceiling.
+| `(alinx_ax7101)`, the LiteX top's own logic | 5,119 | 6,283 | 47 | 10 | 0 | The switch-over and LiteX stages 1 to 3; its 47 RAMB36 are 29 generated FIFOs and 18 BIOS ROM and SRAM ([#640](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/640#issuecomment-6545523624)) |
+| `VexiiRiscvLitex_*`, the SoC CPU | 3,249 | 4,737 | 2 | 5 | 0 | Replaced by the split CPU, the one hart ([lever 4](#lever-4-accounting)) |
+=34,223 the owner's 34.2k (estimate)
+- 3,249 SoC CPU, already in the 52,008 (measured row)
+=30,974 about 31.0k LUTs, CPU counted once (derived)
+### Lever 4 accounting
+The basis already holds the SoC CPU, 3,249 LUTs.
+`MilanSoC` under `--ctrl-mailbox` has always built one hart.
+The split CPU replaces it; no second hart exists.
+The owner's chain counted both CPUs ([#665](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/665#issuecomment-6569635407)).
+| Figure | LUT | Kind | Source |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| SoC CPU in the basis | 3,249 | Measured | Basis hierarchical placement report, row `VexiiRiscvLitex_f5f08b170311db53220574624f819159` |
+| Split CPU, 16 KiB instruction cache, behind the clock crossing | 3,948 | Measured | Out-of-context netlist, [#665 S2b round 12](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/665#issuecomment-6569635407); 3,543 on `sys` without the crossing |
+| The one CPU's net change | +699 | Derived | 3,948 - 3,249 |
+| Start, CPU counted once | 30,974 | Derived | 34,223 - 3,249 |
+| After levers 1 to 3, central | 27,224 | Derived | 30,974 - 1,000 - 1,000 - 1,750 |
+| After lever 4 | 27,224 | Derived | Lever 4 saves 0 |
+| The owner's chain after lever 4, crediting the 3,249 | 27,224 | Derived | 34,223 - 3,750 - 3,249 |
+After lever 4 both chains reach 27,224 LUTs.
+Lever 4 only removed the double count.
+Its middle figure, 3.55k, overstated that by 0.3k.
+So totals from lever 4 on rise about 0.3k.
+### Lever order
+Running totals start from 30,974, CPU counted once.
+They are derived, not the owner's figures.
+| 1 | Drop DDR3, on-chip main memory | Low | Approved (D4). The firmware is already in Block RAM. Also removes the ZQCS timing path | ~1.0k | ~30.0k |
+| 2 | Slimmer LiteX bus and CSR banks | Low to medium | Structural change in the SoC generator, covered by the LiteX simulations. Touches the #814 cuts | ~1.0k | ~29.0k |
+| 3 | `milan_csr` reduced to information and diagnostics | Medium | Many suites, the host tools and the firmware read it. Needs a consumer census first | ~1.75k | ~27.2k |
+| 4 | One CPU (no second hart) | High | S2b round 11 already missed the 8x8 service bounds at 50 MHz. One core adds boot, capture and NVM duties to the protocol load | 0, an [accounting correction](#lever-4-accounting); the owner's table had ~3.55k | ~27.2k |
+| 5 | MMCM servo in firmware | High | Media-clock lock is hard real time. Firmware jitter reaches CRF lock and audio | ~1.0k | ~26.2k |
+| 6 | gPTP to software, hardware timestamps kept | Very high | 802.1AS deadlines, GM recovery and fast reaction. The whole gPTP qualification must be re-run | ~5.2k | ~21.0k |
+The ranges then give 19.8k to 22.4k.
+| Owner levers 1 to 6 | ~21.0k | 19.8k to 22.4k | The owner's ranges from 30,974; lever 4 saves 0 |
+| **32x32 estimate** | **~21.75k** | **18.5k to 25.45k** | Sum |
+Centrally, the image misses the ceiling by about 1.75k.
+| 1 | Drop DDR3 and the BIOS ROM; main memory on chip (D4); our boot loader copies the firmware image from QSPI flash into Block RAM ([D18](#mark-ii-decisions)); the image has its own flash slot ([D21](#mark-ii-decisions)); masters attach to the SoC bus ([stage 1](#stage-1-boot-and-bus)) | LiteDRAM controller and PHY. The BIOS ROM and SRAM, 18 RAMB36 measured, already left at the switch-over (M8a-1, [Mark I ledger](#firmware-and-block-ram-ledger)) | Lever 1, ~1.0k LUTs |
+| 3 | A plain SystemVerilog top replaces the SoC generator | Wishbone and CSR-bank glue; the FIFOs of stage 3 blocks. The 29 generated FIFO RAMB36 leave earlier, each with its block ([census](#litex-block-census)) | Lever 2, ~1.0k, and the rest of the -1.75k |
+Stage 1's Change cell keeps the owner's words.
+The [census](#litex-block-census) places the BIOS at the switch-over.
+The switch-over removes the BIOS and its ROM and SRAM.
+Until stage 3, `ctrl_memory`'s banks hold both regions.
+Stage 1 drops DDR3 and fills `rom` from flash ([D18](#mark-ii-decisions)).
+### Stage 1 boot and bus
+Stage 1's loader copies the firmware image from QSPI flash.
+It copies it into Block RAM, then starts it ([D18](#mark-ii-decisions)).
+The image gets its own flash slot ([D21](#mark-ii-decisions)).
+Its range is free, erase-aligned, never reserved or used.
+The bitstream and AEM slots keep their offsets and budgets.
+No image goes in the bitstream slot's tail.
+The stage 1 lane states the slot's offset and budget.
+They go in `FLASHBOOT_LAYOUT` and the generated layout page.
+Until then, [QSPI_FLASHBOOT.md](../integration/QSPI_FLASHBOOT.md#layout-baremetal---shipping-ax7101) describes the shipping layout.
+Masters use the SoC bus, never the CPU's DMA port.
+The [stage 1 silicon probe](https://github.com/kebag-logic/milan-fpga/issues/640#issuecomment-6556743957) measured the DMA route hanging.
+Through that port, 3 reads were issued and none answered.
+Its cause is not established.
+With the masters on the SoC bus, the entity advertised.
+All 33 descriptors read back byte-identical.
+Both are measurements, not a diagnosis.
+The processor's three masters leave at the switch-over.
+Any later master also attaches to the SoC bus.
+### LiteX block census
+[D19](#mark-ii-decisions) removes every LiteX block, beyond DDR3 and the BIOS.
+Each block below names its stage and its fate.
+Rows follow the basis build's module tree and CSR map.
+LUT figures are the basis's placed rows.
+The switch-over's split build adds the last two rows.
+Every tree node and CSR register maps to one row.
+The LiteX top is one flat row, 5,119 LUTs.
+Its blocks have no placed row of their own.
+No fate below is a LiteX block.
